1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,403
A young man comes first in the whole country in his medical exams.

2
00:00:04,403 --> 00:00:09,308
The first thing he does is touch his father's feet, and the whole neighbourhood praises him for it.

3
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:17,334
Forty years later, the same son, now the richest doctor in town, reaches out to press his old father's feet.

4
00:00:17,334 --> 00:00:20,329
And the father tucks them away, out of reach.

5
00:00:20,329 --> 00:00:21,350
What happened in between?

6
00:00:26,833 --> 00:00:34,119
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 3: A Devoted Son, by the Indian writer Anita Desai.

7
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:37,608
Six parts.

8
00:00:37,608 --> 00:00:39,028
Anita Desai.

9
00:00:39,028 --> 00:00:40,460
The son's rise.

10
00:00:40,460 --> 00:00:42,986
The turn, and the father's decline.

11
00:00:42,986 --> 00:00:44,275
The characters.

12
00:00:44,275 --> 00:00:45,460
The themes.

13
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:50,465
And how the story is built: two halves, a shifting viewpoint, and irony.

14
00:00:54,800 --> 00:01:01,078
Anita Desai, born in nineteen thirty seven, is one of India's leading writers in English.

15
00:01:01,078 --> 00:01:10,960
Her mother was German and her father Bengali; she grew up in Delhi speaking German at home and Hindi outside, and English became her literary language.

16
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:14,372
Three of her novels were shortlisted for the Booker Prize.

17
00:01:14,372 --> 00:01:18,548
This story comes from her collection Games at Twilight, from nineteen seventy eight.

18
00:01:18,767 --> 00:01:27,545
Her subject is the inner life of families: the quiet pressure of duty, and the gap between what people say and what they feel.

19
00:01:27,545 --> 00:01:32,134
This story is also funny, and then, halfway through, very bitter.

20
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:39,422
The first half is a success story.

21
00:01:39,422 --> 00:01:46,622
Rakesh's grandfather sold vegetables; his father Varma worked forty years at a kerosene depot.

22
00:01:46,622 --> 00:01:52,625
Rakesh, the first in the family ever educated, comes first in the country in his exams.

23
00:01:52,625 --> 00:01:58,198
Neighbours crowd in with garlands and sweets, admiring, and a little envious.

24
00:01:58,198 --> 00:02:05,047
He wins a scholarship to the USA, which Varma proudly insists everyone call it, not America.

25
00:02:05,047 --> 00:02:14,617
Then, surprisingly, he comes home, touches his father's feet again, and marries the plump, placid village girl his mother chose, Veena.

26
00:02:14,617 --> 00:02:21,898
He becomes a hospital director, then opens his own clinic, and drives his parents there in a new sky blue Ambassador.

27
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:24,068
Notice the narrator's tone here.

28
00:02:24,068 --> 00:02:32,245
It echoes the neighbours' own over the top praise, and it gently mocks the family's new snobberies, like the USA joke.

29
00:02:32,245 --> 00:02:34,583
It is warm, and it is satire.

30
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:40,788
Then comes the hinge.

31
00:02:40,788 --> 00:02:46,543
Talent and skill, Desai writes, if displayed too long, cease to dazzle.

32
00:02:46,543 --> 00:02:47,962
The tone cools.

33
00:02:47,962 --> 00:02:53,265
Varma has retired, his wife has died, and the old man goes to pieces.

34
00:02:54,467 --> 00:02:57,177
Rakesh takes charge of his father's diet.

35
00:02:57,177 --> 00:03:03,870
After a bout of illness from halwa and cream, the famous doctor forbids anything rich.

36
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:08,818
Fried food goes, then sweets, then everything the old man enjoys.

37
00:03:08,818 --> 00:03:16,278
Varma bribes his grandsons to buy him jalebis, and is caught; Rakesh warns of cholera and typhoid.

38
00:03:16,278 --> 00:03:22,421
What is left: dry bread, boiled lentils, boiled vegetables, and cereal weighed on scales.

39
00:03:23,633 --> 00:03:34,976
On that thin diet Varma weakens, and pills and tonics take the place of food: for his bowels, his blood pressure, his arthritis, and finally to keep his heart beating.

40
00:03:34,976 --> 00:03:39,390
There are rushes to hospital and the humiliation of the stomach pump.

41
00:03:39,390 --> 00:03:43,844
When the old man begs, let me die, his son replies that he has his duty.

42
00:03:45,067 --> 00:03:46,201
The last scene.

43
00:03:46,201 --> 00:03:53,272
On the veranda, Veena props him up on pillows that make his head swim: Rakesh says you can sit if you try.

44
00:03:53,272 --> 00:03:59,925
He stares at the gate, which carries his son's name and degrees; his own name vanished long ago.

45
00:03:59,925 --> 00:04:06,774
The doctor arrives in white with a new tonic and reaches out, ever devoted, to press his father's feet.

46
00:04:06,774 --> 00:04:12,647
The old man tucks them away, and with one grand sweep knocks the bottle from his son's hand.

47
00:04:12,647 --> 00:04:13,988
It smashes.

48
00:04:13,988 --> 00:04:16,787
Brown syrup stains the white trousers.

49
00:04:16,787 --> 00:04:25,011
For once there is noise and attention around him, and he lies back like a prophet and groans that God is calling him: now let him go.

50
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:31,794
Desai is fair to Rakesh.

51
00:04:31,794 --> 00:04:39,372
He is hard working, modest and loyal; he came home when he need not have; nothing shows him neglecting a duty.

52
00:04:39,372 --> 00:04:51,169
But he turns every wish into a medical fact, laughs outside the door at his father's complaints, and cannot hear a plain no, perhaps because everyone has always agreed with him.

53
00:04:51,169 --> 00:04:59,333
Varma is no saint either: vain, greedy for sweets, given to fake deaths, willing to bribe a grandchild.

54
00:04:59,333 --> 00:05:01,197
Yet Desai gives him the last word.

55
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:09,253
Veena we only see through the old man's resentful eyes, so we cannot tell if she is unkind or simply obedient.

56
00:05:09,253 --> 00:05:16,866
And Bhatia, the neighbour over the hedge, is the voice of common sense: shocked that a son could refuse his father food.

57
00:05:21,633 --> 00:05:24,955
The central theme is duty and its limits.

58
00:05:24,955 --> 00:05:32,585
The community's idea of a good son is ritual and visible: touch the feet, obey, provide.

59
00:05:32,585 --> 00:05:40,645
Varma's idea is personal: listen to me, feed me what I love, let me choose how my life ends.

60
00:05:40,645 --> 00:05:44,964
Rakesh fulfils the first completely, and never notices the second.

61
00:05:46,167 --> 00:05:48,226
Around it, more themes.

62
00:05:48,226 --> 00:05:57,288
Care against control: the story never says Rakesh is medically wrong, but asks what a life is worth if it is lived only to eat medicines.

63
00:05:57,288 --> 00:06:08,265
Appearance and reality: the same behaviour is admired in the first half and called tyranny in the second; as the narrator admits, only the interpretation has altered.

64
00:06:08,265 --> 00:06:15,609
Ambition and its cost: in the new prosperity there is no room for the old man's tastes, or even his name.

65
00:06:15,609 --> 00:06:21,248
And old age: Varma does not want more life; he wants his exhaustion respected.

66
00:06:26,033 --> 00:06:28,652
The story has two halves of opposite mood.

67
00:06:28,652 --> 00:06:34,589
In the first, festive and comic, the narrator echoes the neighbours' admiration.

68
00:06:34,589 --> 00:06:43,626
After the hinge, the narrator moves close to Varma and even borrows his words, calling Veena a smiling, hypocritical pudding.

69
00:06:43,626 --> 00:06:47,298
And we are never once taken inside Rakesh's mind.

70
00:06:47,298 --> 00:06:53,663
That is why the ending lands like an accusation: we know what the father feels, and can only guess at the son.

71
00:06:54,867 --> 00:06:59,022
The main tool is irony, in all three kinds.

72
00:06:59,022 --> 00:07:04,999
Verbal irony: such a son as few women had borne, praise with a mocking edge.

73
00:07:04,999 --> 00:07:10,897
Situational irony: each kindness of the son increases the father's misery.

74
00:07:10,897 --> 00:07:17,408
Dramatic irony: we know the old man means it when he says he is dying; his family thinks he is acting.

75
00:07:17,408 --> 00:07:23,021
And above all, the title, which means one thing in the first half and the opposite in the second.

76
00:07:24,233 --> 00:07:25,732
Devices to quote.

77
00:07:25,732 --> 00:07:34,977
The motif of feet: touched in respect at the start, tucked away in refusal at the end; Desai never comments, the reversal does it.

78
00:07:34,977 --> 00:07:39,156
Symbols: the signboard with the son's name, and the tonic bottle.

79
00:07:39,156 --> 00:07:43,349
Similes that make the old man grand, like some dire prophet.

80
00:07:43,349 --> 00:07:45,107
Satire on snobbery.

81
00:07:45,107 --> 00:07:51,227
And the contrast between the old world of halwa and betel and the new world of scales and pills.

82
00:07:53,633 --> 00:07:54,404
Pause and think.

83
00:08:01,700 --> 00:08:08,313
Rakesh does every duty, yet the care takes away his father's choices, food and dignity.

84
00:08:10,300 --> 00:08:10,735
One more.

85
00:08:18,367 --> 00:08:24,178
It moves from echoing the community's praise of Rakesh to sharing Varma's feelings and words.

86
00:08:26,367 --> 00:08:27,462
Quick recap.

87
00:08:27,462 --> 00:08:29,871
Desai, and Games at Twilight.

88
00:08:29,871 --> 00:08:31,512
The son's great ascent.

89
00:08:31,512 --> 00:08:34,442
The hinge, and a diet that becomes a cell.

90
00:08:34,442 --> 00:08:37,254
Duty against will, care against control.

91
00:08:37,254 --> 00:08:41,763
And the irony of a devoted son whose father finally tucks his feet away.

92
00:08:45,933 --> 00:08:47,471
That was A Devoted Son.

93
00:08:47,471 --> 00:08:55,192
Next, a tale of greed and gold on a tropical island: The Treasure in the Forest, by H. G. Wells.

94
00:08:55,192 --> 00:08:55,740
See you there.

